WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.220
Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2 crashed into
theaters with all the action and comedy fans

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.690
of the franchise have been waiting for — not
to mention dozens of in-jokes and callbacks

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:12.040
to both the previous film and the comics.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:13.870
If you didn't spot them all, don't worry!

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:17.439
We picked out some of the most interesting
and obscure Easter eggs in Guardians of the

00:00:17.439 --> 00:00:18.439
Galaxy 2.

00:00:18.439 --> 00:00:21.339
Fair warning: Major spoilers coming up.

00:00:21.339 --> 00:00:22.660
Star-Lord's shirt

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:27.529
Guardians is full of tiny details, which is
part of what makes the films so fun to watch.

00:00:27.529 --> 00:00:31.820
But one of the weirdest questions in Guardians
Vol. 2 got answered before the movie even

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:32.850
came out.

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:37.660
Several of the trailers show Peter Quill wearing
a shirt with a weird, alien-looking logo.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:39.840
To most people, it was just a cool looking
shirt.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.750
But to Guardians super-fans, it was a challenge.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:47.039
As one Reddit user figured out, the graphic
on the front of the shirt actually says "Gears

00:00:47.039 --> 00:00:51.870
Shift" in an alien language — the language
used on a keyboard in the first film.

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:56.790
Exactly what that means is still a mystery,
but hey, it's pretty cool regardless.

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:58.190
The Kronan connection

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:02.910
As Rocket and Yondu are portal-jumping across
planets to reach Ego, we get to see a ton

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:07.210
of interesting visuals — like the legendary
Stan Lee talking to a group of Watchers.

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:12.060
But one other cool glimpse we got was of a
barren alien planet with two hulking aliens

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:13.470
beating each other up.

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:19.020
Those guys look a lot like Kronans, a technologically
advanced, militant race of beings that look

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:21.290
sort of like big, humanoid rocks.

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:22.369
So what's the connection?

00:01:22.369 --> 00:01:27.490
Well, we've already seen a Kronan in the Marvel
Cinematic Universe in Thor: The Dark World.

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:32.210
That guy got blasted into gravel, but Thor:
Ragnarok will feature another Kronan — Korg,

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:35.600
who in the comics becomes the Hulk's ally.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.480
Korg's role in Ragnarok is still unknown,
but now that Guardians took the time to feature

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:46.009
the race of rock men, we have to wonder: Was
Korg one of those figures fighting in Guardians?

00:01:46.009 --> 00:01:50.909
Or was that shot simply put in as a bonus
for any fan paying close attention, with no

00:01:50.909 --> 00:01:53.850
bearing on the rest of the MCU?

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:55.060
Cosmo returns

00:01:55.060 --> 00:02:00.170
One of the weirdest characters from the comics
has now made an appearance in both Guardians

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:01.170
films.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:04.880
We're talking, of course, about Cosmo, the
space-suited dog who runs up and licks the

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:07.729
Collector in the post credits scene from the
first film.

00:02:07.729 --> 00:02:12.300
Cosmo showed up a few times before that in
the Collector's, uh, collection.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:16.400
And just like Howard the Duck, this guy was
back for another round in the post-credits

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:17.830
scenes of Guardians 2.

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:21.590
At first, this Easter egg seems like a simple
callback to the first film.

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:25.681
But what you may not know is that Cosmo is
actually a recurring character in the Guardians'

00:02:25.681 --> 00:02:26.681
comics and cartoons.

00:02:26.681 --> 00:02:30.379
And he does a lot more than lick people when
they're sad.

00:02:30.379 --> 00:02:36.040
Cosmo is a...well, he's a telepathic space
dog who acts as the head of security for Knowhere.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:40.349
That's where we meet the Collector in the
first film, and he's incredibly powerful.

00:02:40.349 --> 00:02:46.400
For example, in Thanos Imperative #2, Cosmos
singlehandedly killed the Hulk with a mental

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:51.470
shockwave, and he once went toe-to-toe with
Adam Warlock and came out on top.

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:54.220
This is one doggy you don't want to mess with.

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:57.040
And while we're hanging out on Knowhere...

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:58.090
Celestial trifecta

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:02.250
Ego's reveal as a Celestial was pretty big
news in Guardians 2.

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:06.519
If you don't know, the Celestials are an extremely
powerful cosmic race.

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:09.830
They're basically space gods, as Ego himself
said in the movie.

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:13.870
But this isn't the first time we've seen a
Celestial in the Guardians of the Galaxy films

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:16.220
— it's actually the third time.

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:20.290
The one most fans know about popped up in
the first Guardians when Star-Lord and his

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:22.790
troupe went to Knowhere to meet with the Collector.

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:27.370
While explaining the Infinity Stones, Collector
shows them a video of a big metal man with

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:29.590
the purple one, blasting people into dust.

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:39.400
"These carriers can use the stone to mow down
entire civilizations like wheat in a field."

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:43.580
That guy was basically a photocopy of the
Marvel Comics Celestials.

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:45.090
And the third Celestial?

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:46.990
Well, it's in the same scene.

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:48.560
They were all standing on it.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.640
In it, to be exact.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:55.129
Knowhere — the Collector's little black
market mining colony — is the severed head

00:03:55.129 --> 00:03:56.660
of a Celestial.

00:03:56.660 --> 00:04:01.700
"It's called Knowhere, the severed head of
an ancient Celestial being."

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:05.890
So when Ego revealed that he was a Celestial,
the idea should've been pretty much old-hat

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:08.409
to the fans who've been keeping up with things
the whole time.

00:04:08.409 --> 00:04:10.170
Yondu's big orange fin

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:14.470
Michael Rooker's Yondu looks pretty close
to the Yondu of the comics — he's a big

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:15.470
blue guy.

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:17.260
It's a pretty hard look to screw up.

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:19.140
And yet there is a big difference.

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:25.470
In the comics, Yondu's head-fin is a big orange
mohawk, not the slim tangerine curve of the

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:26.470
first film.

00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:31.760
Well, until Yondu replaces his broken fin
with a prototype version in Guardians 2 — a

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:34.720
prototype that's a bit bigger and much...oranger.

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:41.090
It still doesn't come close to the glorious
head-spanning monstrosity of the early comics,

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:45.320
but Yondu's new look takes him one step closer
to his comic incarnation.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:46.700
The crystal frog

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:51.250
Yondu's little crystal frog practically had
a subplot of its own in the first Guardians,

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:53.340
although most people completely missed it.

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:56.130
"You got any other cute little buggers like
this'n?

00:04:56.130 --> 00:04:59.720
I like to stick 'em all in a row on my control
console."

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:02.450
"I can't tell if you're joking or not."

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:03.450
Yeah, that thing.

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:07.060
Well, Yondu got his frog, and he kept it with
him.

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:11.410
Before he goes into the climactic fight in
the skies of Xandar, that little frog is sitting

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:12.670
right by his hand.

00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:17.060
And when his ship crashes on the planet, the
crystal frog is the only piece of wreckage

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:19.420
Yondu picks up out of the dirt.

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:21.840
Unfortunately, Yondu bit the big one.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:23.320
But he still has his frog.

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:28.990
In Guardians 2, during the overhead shot of
Yondu's funeral pyre, his body is surrounded

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:30.940
by ornaments of his life.

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:35.570
And one of those symbolic trinkets is — you
guessed it — that little blue frog.

00:05:35.570 --> 00:05:40.400
We can only assume that frog has been to hell
and back again at Yondu's side, so it's only

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:45.590
fitting that that's where it remains when
Yondu is blasted into space in a million technicolor

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:46.590
bits.

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:47.590
Thanks for watching!

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:49.900
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:51.930
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

